  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe current algorithm uses a continuous relaxation and local search methods to minimize the maximum overlap integral. The new approach focuses on **binary representations** and **sparse optimization** while leveraging global reparameterization through **Fourier analysis** and **convex programming**. The idea is to model the step function as a linear combination of Fourier modes, which provides a smooth representation that can be optimized efficiently. The key insight is that the Fourier basis captures periodicity constraints inherent in the problem, enabling faster convergence to high-quality solutions.\n\nThe new approach addresses the limitations of the current method by:\n- Switching from a continuous optimization in the spatial domain to a frequency-domain optimization with Fourier coefficients\n- Maintaining feasibility constraints using projected gradient steps and penalty functions\n- Exploiting sparsity in the Fourier representation to reduce problem dimension\n- Using convex relaxations when possible to guarantee better local optima quality\n\nThe main bottlenecks are the non-convex nature of the problem and the need to enforce [0,1] constraints. The solution balances these by combining alternating projections onto the feasible set with gradient-based optimization.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nRepresent the step function h(x) as a periodic function with N=240 samples:\n    h(x) = (1/2) + \u03a3_{k=-N/2}^{N/2-1} a_k e^{-i 2\u03c0k x / 2}\nwith h(x) \u2208 [0,1].\n\nUse Fourier coefficients a_k \u2208 \u211d for optimization. The integral constraint becomes:\n    \u03a3_{i=0}^{N-1} h[i]/N = 0.5\n\nThe overlap integral for shift k is:\n    I(k) = (1/N) * \u03a3_{i=0}^{N-1} h[i] * (1 - h[i+k % N])\n\nConstraint processing:\n    For each iterative update: \n        1. Update Fourier coefficients using gradient descent\n        2. Invert Fourier transform to get real space h\n        3. Project back to [0,1] using clipping followed by scaling\n        4. Re-project coefficient distribution to satisfy integral constraint\n\nJustification: This ensures both periodicity (handled by the basis functions) and the [0,1] constraint, while leveraging the smoothing properties of the Fourier basis.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nA. Validation and parameterization:\n    - Read initial_h_values (n_points=240) if available\n    - Convert to Fourier coefficients using RFFT\n    - Initialize coefficients with uniform distribution (weight=0.01 per coefficient)\n\nB. Optimization loop (budget ~90%):\n    - Phase1: Fourier-space gradient descent\n        For each iteration (1200 steps):\n            * Compute gradient of max(I(k)) w.r.t. Fourier coefficients\n            * Update using momentum optimization\n            * Apply saturation and projection for [0,1] constraint\n            * Dynamic step size adjustment based on gradient magnitude\n\n    - Phase2: Coarse-to-fine refinement\n        Reduce spatial resolution to 48, 96, 192 samples:\n        * Solve with N=48 binary trials (each optimization uses projected gradient)\n        * Refine solution by interpolating and continuing optimization\n        * Track best solution across all resolutions\n\nC. Local search polishing (budget ~10%):\n    - After global optimization, perform 200 iterations of:\n        Local coordinate descent on h space with ADAM optimizer\n        Quadratic projection to ensure [0,1] feasibility\n    - Apply final clipping and rescaling if needed\n\nD. Feasibility restoration:\n    - If any h[i] < 0, use L2 projection to the nearest feasible point\n    - If integral constraint violated, silence with noise-reducing projection\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Starting configurations:\n  1. Fourier-initialization (default, has shown ~0.385)\n  2. Sparse initialization (use only odd harmonics, reduces variance)\n\n- Resolution progression:\n  30 trials using N=48,96,192,240 points\n  Each run initializes with random phase and amplitude\n\n- Parameter tuning:\n  * Momentum coefficient: [0.95, 0.99]\n  * Initial learning rate: 0.5 (decreased by 0.9 per 600 steps)\n  * Saturation threshold: 1e-3 for clipping\n\n- Important variations:\n  A. Multi-population: Simultaneously run 4 different random seeds\n  B. Sparsity control: Add elastic net (L1-L2) penalties on coefficients\n  C. Warm-start continuation: Save/checkpoint intermediate Fourier bases\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Resolution strategy:\n  T1=55% budget: N=48 optimization (1500 sec estimate)\n  T2=10% budget: N=240 optimization\n  T3=15% budget: Multiple continuation trials\n\n- Termination checks:\n  * Restart every 400 iterations if no improvement\n  * Verify solution at end with tolerance 1e-06\n  * Save best solution every 500 iterations\n\n- Constraint handling:\n  * Gradient projection: min(max(h[i],0),1)\n  * Penalty terms for integral: sum(h)^2 to Lagrangian\n  * When skipping workers, use fallback projection\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nPre-optimization checks:\n  - Verify dimensionality: n_points even\n  - Compute initial_c5 using provided compute_c5\n\nPost-optimization checks:\n  1. Validate integral: project_to_sum(h_target, n_points/2) \n  2. Ensure all values: 0<=h[i]<=1\n  3. Recompute c5 using task evaluator\n\nFinal return format:\n  - h_values: np.array with length n_points\n  - c5_bound: float rounded to 6 decimal places\n  - n_points: 240 (if initial provided) or most recent resolution\n\nHandle abrupt termination:\n  - Save best_valid = (h, c5, n_points) before every evaluated state\n  - Use fallback parent on restart (if available)",
